# NativeAOT and trimming

Apps that use HyperPlot may ship as NativeAOT; development runs on the JIT (design §0.1). Both must behave the same, so library code is written for NativeAOT first.

- Every project under `src/` is `IsAotCompatible`. The trim, AOT, and single-file analyzers run in every build, and their warnings are errors.
- The exception is `HyperPlot.Wpf`: WPF itself cannot be NativeAOT-published, so the adapter sets `IsAotCompatible` to `false` in its own project file and runs on the JIT only. It still holds to every other line of this rule: no reflection of its own, no dynamic code, and nothing the engine projects need goes into it. Everything below Wpf (Core and Rendering) stays publishable without it.
- No reflection that the trimmer cannot see (design §11, invariant 9): `Type.GetType(string)`, `Activator.CreateInstance(Type)`, `MakeGenericType`, `MakeGenericMethod`, `GetMethod`/`GetProperty` by name, `System.Reflection.Emit`, `Expression.Compile`. Code that needs a type-driven table (a column of `T`, a spec field bound to a record's member) is generated by a Source Generator or written by hand.
- No `[DynamicallyAccessedMembers]`, `[RequiresUnreferencedCode]`, `[RequiresDynamicCode]`, or `[UnconditionalSuppressMessage]` to silence an analyzer. If one seems needed, the design is wrong for AOT; raise it on the Issue.
- Serialization, if any (a spec read from JSON, a column file's header), uses source-generated serializers (System.Text.Json source generation or similar), never reflection-based ones.
- Interop uses `LibraryImport` and unmanaged function pointers, not `DllImport` with runtime marshalling or `ComImport` RCWs (`.cursor/rules/interop.mdc`).
- Generic virtual methods and generic interfaces instantiated over many value types grow the binary (a column type per element type, a mark per encoding). When one is added on a public type, the PR reports the size of the NativeAOT sample before and after. A switch that names each element type to make a generic object (`Column<float> c => new X<float>(c)` to `Column<byte>`) compiles every type's chain (its pyramids, its M4) into every application, built or not; a virtual method on the generic type (`Column<T>` returns `new X<T>(this)`) compiles only the types an application builds. PR #173's gathers grew `SpecPlot` by about 121 KB the first way and 13 KB the second.
- A Source Generator ships inside the package that uses it (`analyzers/dotnet/cs`), not as a separate package.

## The check

A PR that adds or changes a `src/` project's public surface, a dependency, or interop runs a NativeAOT publish of the sample its Issue names. That sample uses Core and `HyperPlot.Rendering.D3D11` without WPF (headless, design §7.5: `samples/HelloPlot` draws offscreen and writes a PNG), so that the WPF exception cannot hide a warning of the engine. A row that comes before its sample exists (R1, before `HelloPlot` in S1) publishes a scratch program that uses the row's types instead, states its warnings and size, and does not keep it (`.cursor/rules/scratch.mdc`):

The feat Issue of a row that changes the public surface, a dependency, or interop names the NativeAOT publish in its Scope. PR #47's Issue did not, and the publish was left until the review asked.

```powershell
$env:PATH += ';C:\Program Files (x86)\Microsoft Visual Studio\Installer'
dotnet publish samples/<Sample> -c Release -r win-x64 -o <scratch>
<scratch>/<Sample>.exe --auto
```

Under `dotnet run`, a project with `PublishAot` reports `RuntimeFeature.IsDynamicCodeSupported` and `IsDynamicCodeCompiled` as false too. To tell the builds apart at run time, use `System.Runtime.JitInfo.GetCompiledMethodCount() > 0` (JIT).

The PR's `## Verification` states the warning count (zero) and the size of the executable.

- An Issue whose Acceptance names only `IsAotCompatible` does not replace this check. PR #82 marked the publish N/A for that reason, and the review asked for it.
- When the sample exists but does not use the row's new public types, publish both: the sample on `main` and on the head, for the size it grew, and a scratch program that uses the types (the design's example of the row), for their warnings and size.
- The build of `main` for the size before is a worktree under `.dev/` (`.cursor/rules/bench.mdc`). In the scratchpad, `git worktree remove` fails with `Filename too long`.
- To split a size, publish both builds with `-p:IlcGenerateMapFile=true`. Then sum the `Length` of the nodes of each `*.map.xml` (under `obj/`) by `Name`, and compare the two sums name by name.

A size that grew is split by what added it before the PR says so: publish with `-p:IlcGenerateMapFile=true` and compare the map files. Source-generated COM can link the reflection runtime and its metadata (about 0.4 MB); a `Process` or LINQ added only to print figures can be most of a sample's growth. A sample whose size is reported gets the feature it shows and nothing else; figures that need more code are measured in `benchmarks/`.

```text
# BAD — the AOT check publishes the WPF sample with IsAotCompatible=false, and reports "0 warnings"
# GOOD — the AOT check publishes the Core + D3D11 sample: 0 warnings, 3.1 MB; the WPF sample runs on the JIT only
```
